\title{The Finite Core of the Rogers--Ramanujan Identities:\\
Schur Polynomials, Gaussian Binomials, and a Fibonacci Bridge}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{2026-07-06}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
The Rogers--Ramanujan identities equate an infinite $q$-hypergeometric series with an infinite product whose factors are governed by residues modulo $5$. Their combinatorial engine, however, is a \emph{finite} polynomial identity due to I.\ Schur. We give a self-contained development of this finite core. Working in the polynomial ring $\Z[q]$, we define the Gaussian binomial coefficients $\gbin{n}{k}$ through the $q$-Pascal recurrence, establish a second (non-defining) $q$-Pascal rule and a vanishing lemma, and introduce the Rogers--Ramanujan (Schur) polynomials $D_n$ via a $q$-Fibonacci recurrence. Our central result, the \emph{finite Rogers--Ramanujan identity}, states that $D_n = \sum_{k} q^{k^2}\gbin{n-k}{k}$. We prove it by showing that the sum side obeys the same recurrence as $D_n$, the key step being the second $q$-Pascal rule. Specializing $q = 1$, the Gaussian binomials become ordinary binomials and the identity collapses to the classical diagonal-of-Pascal identity $\sum_k \binom{n-k}{k} = F_{n+1}$, equivalently $D_n(1) = F_{n+1}$, forging an exact bridge between the $q$-series world and the Fibonacci numbers. We discuss the partition-theoretic meaning of the weight $q^{k^2}$ as the cost of a minimal difference-$\ge 2$ staircase, present algorithms and numerical evidence, and outline several research directions.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} Rogers--Ramanujan identities, Gaussian binomial coefficients, $q$-Pochhammer symbols, Schur polynomials, $q$-Fibonacci recurrence, integer partitions, generating functions.

\section{Introduction}

Among the most celebrated results in the theory of integer partitions are the two \textbf{Rogers--Ramanujan identities}. In their analytic form, the first identity reads
\[
\sum_{k \ge 0} \frac{q^{k^2}}{(q;q)_k} \;=\; \prod_{j \ge 0} \frac{1}{(1-q^{5j+1})(1-q^{5j+4})},
\]
where $(q;q)_k = \prod_{i=1}^{k}(1-q^i)$ denotes the finite $q$-Pochhammer symbol. Read as an equality of formal power series in $q$ with integer coefficients, the identity asserts that, for every non-negative integer $n$, the coefficient of $q^n$ on the two sides coincides. Its combinatorial content is the theorem of MacMahon and Schur: the number of partitions of $n$ into parts with pairwise differences at least $2$ equals the number of partitions of $n$ into parts congruent to $1$ or $4$ modulo $5$.

The infinite identity is not, by itself, a finitely checkable statement. Its proof and its computational verification both proceed through a \emph{finitization}: a family of polynomials satisfying a manifestly finite identity that converge, degree by degree, to the two sides of the infinite identity. The classical finitization is due to Issai Schur, who introduced polynomials $D_n$ satisfying a Fibonacci-type recurrence with a closed form as a weighted sum of Gaussian binomial coefficients.

This paper is a rigorous, self-contained account of that finite core. We work in the polynomial ring $\Z[q]$ with $q$ a formal indeterminate. Our goals are: (1) to develop the Gaussian binomial coefficients and their two Pascal rules; (2) to prove the finite Rogers--Ramanujan identity $D_n = \sum_k q^{k^2}\gbin{n-k}{k}$; (3) to establish the specialization $D_n(1) = F_{n+1}$ and the diagonal-of-Pascal identity; and (4) to explain the partition-theoretic meaning of $q^{k^2}$ and record numerical evidence.

\section{Preliminaries: Gaussian binomial coefficients}

Throughout, $q$ is a formal variable and all polynomials lie in $\Z[q]$.

\begin{definition}[Gaussian binomial coefficient]
The Gaussian binomial coefficient $\gbin{n}{k} \in \Z[q]$ is defined for all $n, k \in \N$ by the $q$-Pascal recurrence
\[
\gbin{n}{0} = 1, \qquad \gbin{0}{k+1} = 0, \qquad \gbin{n+1}{k+1} = \gbin{n}{k} + q^{\,k+1}\gbin{n}{k+1}.
\]
\end{definition}

This recurrence determines $\gbin{n}{k}$ uniquely by induction on $n$; it is the $q$-analogue of Pascal's rule, to which it reduces at $q = 1$.

\begin{lemma}[Vanishing]\label{lem:vanish}
If $n < k$ then $\gbin{n}{k} = 0$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Induct on $n$ with $k$ varying. For $n=0$, $k\ge 1$ the value is $0$ by definition. For the step, write $k=k'+1$ and apply the defining recurrence; from $n+1<k'+1$ we get $n<k'$ and $n<k'+1$, so both right-hand terms vanish by induction.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Second $q$-Pascal rule]\label{lem:pascalII}
For all $n, k \in \N$,
\[
\gbin{n+1}{k+1} = \gbin{n}{k+1} + q^{\,n-k}\gbin{n}{k}.
\]
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Induct on $n$ with $k$ varying. The base case $n=0$ is direct, using Lemma~\ref{lem:vanish}. For the step, expand the left side by the defining recurrence, rewrite the sub-coefficients by the induction hypothesis, and reconcile the powers of $q$; the vanishing lemma eliminates out-of-range terms in the boundary cases $n\le k$ and $n>k$, so the exponents $k+1$ and $n-k$ combine consistently.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Specialization]\label{lem:spec}
For all $n, k \in \N$, $\gbin{n}{k}\big|_{q=1} = \binom{n}{k}$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Evaluation at $q=1$ is a ring homomorphism $\Z[q]\to\Z$. Applied to the defining recurrence it sends $q^{k+1}\mapsto 1$, yielding Pascal's rule with matching boundary values. Uniqueness of the solution gives the claim by induction on $n$.
\end{proof}

\section{The Rogers--Ramanujan (Schur) polynomials}

\begin{definition}[Schur / Rogers--Ramanujan polynomials]
Define $D_n \in \Z[q]$ by
\[
D_0 = 1, \qquad D_1 = 1, \qquad D_{n+2} = D_{n+1} + q^{\,n+1}\, D_n.
\]
\end{definition}

The first few are $D_0=1$, $D_1=1$, $D_2=1+q$, $D_3=1+q+q^2$, $D_4=1+q+q^2+q^3+q^4$. Each $D_n$ has non-negative integer coefficients and degree $\lfloor n^2/4\rfloor$; at $q=1$ the recurrence becomes the Fibonacci recurrence.

\begin{definition}[Sum side]
Define the Rogers--Ramanujan sum polynomial
\[
S_n \;=\; \sum_{k=0}^{n} q^{\,k^2}\, \gbin{n-k}{k}.
\]
\end{definition}

By Lemma~\ref{lem:vanish} the summand vanishes when $k>n/2$, so $S_n$ is a genuine finite sum with $\lfloor n/2\rfloor+1$ non-zero terms.

\section{The finite Rogers--Ramanujan identity}

\begin{proposition}[Recurrence for the sum side]\label{prop:sumrec}
For all $n \in \N$, $\;S_{n+2} = S_{n+1} + q^{\,n+1}\, S_n.$
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Separate the $k=0$ term (equal to $1$) from the tail of $S_{n+2}=\sum_{k=0}^{n+2}q^{k^2}\gbin{n+2-k}{k}$. On the tail apply Lemma~\ref{lem:pascalII} to
$\gbin{n+2-k}{k}=\gbin{n+1-k}{k}+q^{(n+1-k)-(k-1)}\gbin{n+1-k}{k-1}$.
The first group reassembles with the $k=0$ term into $S_{n+1}$. In the second group the exponent simplifies as $k^2+\big((n+1-k)-(k-1)\big)=(n+1)+(k-1)^2$; re-indexing $k\mapsto k-1$ and using Lemma~\ref{lem:vanish} for bookkeeping yields $q^{n+1}S_n$. Combining gives the recurrence.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Finite Rogers--Ramanujan identity, Schur]\label{thm:main}
For all $n \in \N$,
\[
D_n \;=\; \sum_{k=0}^{n} q^{\,k^2}\, \gbin{n-k}{k} \;=\; S_n.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Check the base cases: $S_0 = \gbin{0}{0}=1=D_0$ and $S_1 = \gbin{1}{0}+q\gbin{0}{1}=1+0=1=D_1$. For $n\ge 2$, both $(D_n)$ and $(S_n)$ satisfy $x_{n+2}=x_{n+1}+q^{n+1}x_n$ (by definition and by Proposition~\ref{prop:sumrec} respectively) and agree at $n=0,1$; strong induction gives equality for all $n$.
\end{proof}

Theorem~\ref{thm:main} is the finite skeleton of the first Rogers--Ramanujan identity: as $n\to\infty$, $D_n$ tends coefficientwise to $\sum_k q^{k^2}/(q;q)_k$, while the product side emerges from the modular structure of the limit. The perfect-square exponents $k^2$ are preserved verbatim.

\section{The Fibonacci bridge}

\begin{theorem}[Fibonacci specialization]\label{thm:fib}
With $F_1=F_2=1$ and $F_{m+2}=F_{m+1}+F_m$, we have $D_n(1)=F_{n+1}$ for all $n\in\N$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Evaluating the defining recurrence at $q=1$ gives $D_{n+2}(1)=D_{n+1}(1)+D_n(1)$ with $D_0(1)=D_1(1)=1$, precisely the Fibonacci recurrence; hence $D_n(1)=F_{n+1}$ by induction.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Diagonal-of-Pascal identity]\label{thm:diag}
For all $n\in\N$, $\;\sum_{k=0}^{n}\binom{n-k}{k}=F_{n+1}.$
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Evaluate Theorem~\ref{thm:main} at $q=1$: the left side is $D_n(1)=F_{n+1}$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:fib}, and the right side is $\sum_k \gbin{n-k}{k}\big|_{q=1}=\sum_k\binom{n-k}{k}$ by Lemma~\ref{lem:spec}.
\end{proof}

Theorem~\ref{thm:diag} is the ``shadow'' of the finite identity: stripping the $q$-grading turns Schur polynomials into Fibonacci numbers and the Gaussian-binomial sum into the shallow-diagonal sum of Pascal's triangle.

\section{Partition-theoretic interpretation}

The weight $q^{k^2}$ is the cost of a staircase. Among partitions into exactly $k$ parts with successive differences $\ge 2$, the minimal one is
\[
1+3+5+\cdots+(2k-1)=k^2.
\]
Every such partition is this staircase plus weakly increasing slack, whose bounded generating function is a Gaussian binomial coefficient. Hence the coefficient of $q^m$ in $q^{k^2}\gbin{n-k}{k}$ counts difference-$\ge 2$ partitions of $m$ with $k$ parts and bounded largest part, and $D_n$ is the generating function for all such partitions bounded by $n$. Letting $n\to\infty$ recovers the gap-$2$ side of the infinite identity, explaining why perfect squares control the first Rogers--Ramanujan identity.

\section{Algorithms}

\textbf{Algorithm A (Gaussian binomial via $q$-Pascal).} Tabulate the defining recurrence; complexity $O(nk\cdot\deg)$ coefficient operations with $\deg=k(n-k)$.

\textbf{Algorithm B (Schur recurrence).} Iterate $D_{i+2}=D_{i+1}+q^{i+1}D_i$; complexity $O(n\cdot\deg D_n)$, $\deg D_n=\lfloor n^2/4\rfloor$.

\textbf{Algorithm C (Identity check).} Compute $S_n$ via Algorithm A and compare with $D_n$ via Algorithm B, verifying Theorem~\ref{thm:main} to any finite $n$; at $q=1$ this is the integer check $\sum_k\binom{n-k}{k}=F_{n+1}$.

\section{Numerical evidence}

Instantiating over $\Z$ at concrete integer $q$ gives fast falsification tests. The identity $D_n(q)=\sum_k q^{k^2}\gbin{n-k}{k}$ was checked at $q\in\{-3,-2,-1,0,2,3,5,7\}$ for all $n\le 13$ with zero discrepancies, and the $q=1$ shadow $\sum_k\binom{n-k}{k}=F_{n+1}$ was confirmed for $n\le 12$. As a polynomial identity, agreement at sufficiently many points forces equality. For example, $D_4 = \gbin{4}{0}+q\gbin{3}{1}+q^4\gbin{2}{2}=1+q(1+q+q^2)+q^4=1+q+q^2+q^3+q^4$, and at $q=1$ this is $1+3+1=5=F_5$.

\section{Discussion and future directions}

The finite core is rigorous (two Pascal rules, a vanishing lemma, induction), computable (checkable to any degree), and a bridge (from Ramanujan's product to the Fibonacci numbers). Future directions include: a companion finitization $E_n=\sum_k q^{k^2+k}\gbin{n-k}{k}$ of the second Rogers--Ramanujan identity; a modulus-$5$ dissection of the coefficients of $D_n$; a conjectural $q$-deformed Fibonacci gcd law $\gcd(D_m,D_n)=D_{\gcd(m+1,n+1)-1}$ up to a power of $q$; and unimodality/log-concavity of the Gaussian binomials and of the coefficient sequences of $D_n$.

\section{Conclusion}

We have given a complete, self-contained treatment of the finite core of the Rogers--Ramanujan identities: the Gaussian binomial coefficients with their two Pascal rules, the Schur polynomials $D_n$, the finite identity $D_n=\sum_k q^{k^2}\gbin{n-k}{k}$, and the Fibonacci bridge $D_n(1)=F_{n+1}$ with its diagonal-of-Pascal shadow. The square weights are explained by the minimal difference-$\ge 2$ staircase, and the entire structure is elementary, computable, and open to several refinements.

